## The Ambassadors: what is on the floor?
sources:
    - id: ng
      title: The Ambassadors (NG1314)
      authors: The National Gallery, London
      year: 2024
      finding: Sitters, objects, the hymn book, the lute's broken string, the crucifix and the skull seen from the right
      url: https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/hans-holbein-the-younger-the-ambassadors
    - id: foister
      title: 'Making & Meaning: Holbein''s Ambassadors'
      authors: Susan Foister, Ashok Roy and Martin Wyld
      year: 1997
      finding: The National Gallery's study of the painting, its objects and its making

    image art cites ["ng"] credit "Holbein, 1533. National Gallery, London. Public domain." x 440 y 352 width 665 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Hans_Holbein_the_Younger_-_The_Ambassadors_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Hans_Holbein_the_Younger_-_The_Ambassadors_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.crucifix [0,0.01,0.022,0.11] regions.dagger [0.16,0.52,0.12,0.05] regions.dinteville [0.015,0.09,0.41,0.83] regions.earth_globe [0.41,0.548,0.088,0.085] regions.floor [0,0.76,1,0.24] regions.hat_badge [0.266,0.096,0.018,0.016] regions.hymnbook [0.544,0.634,0.156,0.058] regions.lower_shelf [0.395,0.57,0.37,0.13] regions.lute [0.505,0.57,0.26,0.13] regions.lute_neck [0.515,0.585,0.07,0.06] regions.selve [0.665,0.12,0.33,0.78] regions.selve_book [0.645,0.318,0.065,0.025] regions.smear [0.2,0.76,0.53,0.24] regions.star_globe [0.365,0.19,0.125,0.15] regions.sundials [0.5,0.12,0.19,0.22] regions.upper_shelf [0.365,0.12,0.33,0.24] hidden true
    text w_heavens "Heavens" #2f6b8f x 893 y 530 font_size 44 hidden true
    text w_earth "Earth" #87a878 x 893 y 270 font_size 44 hidden true
    text w_floor "Floor" #b5482e x 893 y 130 font_size 44 hidden true
    portrait holbein of "Hans Holbein the Younger" cameo true hidden true
    image skull credit "Un-stretched by Thomas Shahan (public domain)" x 420 y 205 width 250 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Holbein_Skull.jpg/960px-Holbein_Skull.jpg look screen view [0.18,0.06,0.82,0.86] reveal develop hidden true

    card title "The Ambassadors"

This is The Ambassadors, painted in London in 1533.
    draw art

Its painter, Hans Holbein, was a German from Augsburg who made his name in London, painting the court of Henry the Eighth.
    draw holbein

    erase holbein

And our question is: what is that strange grey smear across the floor doing in it?
    point at.ref art:smear gesture glow

At first sight, it's a show-off portrait: two rich, clever young men, life size.
    highlight art:dinteville art:selve

On the left stands Jean de Dinteville, the French king's ambassador to England.
    highlight art:dinteville

    camera on ["art:dagger"] zoom 3

Holbein even wrote his age on the sheath of his dagger: twenty-nine.
    highlight art:dagger

    camera reset true

His friend Georges de Selve, a young bishop, leans on a book that gives his own age: twenty-five.
    highlight art:selve art:selve_book

Between them stand two shelves, and the whole picture is built in three layers.
    highlight art:upper_shelf art:lower_shelf

First the heavens, then the earth, and last of all, the floor.
    draw w_heavens w_earth w_floor

    camera on ["art:upper_shelf"]

The top shelf is the heavens: a globe of the stars, and instruments for telling the time by the sun.
    highlight art:star_globe art:sundials

    camera on ["art:lower_shelf"]

The lower shelf comes down to earth, with a globe on which Dinteville's own home, Polisy, is marked.
    point at.ref art:earth_globe gesture glow

    camera on ["art:lute_neck"] zoom 5

Next to it lies a lute, and one of its strings has snapped.
    highlight art:lute_neck

    camera on ["art:lower_shelf"]

Beside the lute lies a Lutheran hymn book — a striking thing to put next to a Catholic bishop, just as Henry the Eighth was breaking with Rome.
    highlight art:hymnbook

A broken string beside the songs of a church splitting in two: many read it as a picture of discord, though Holbein left no note to say so.
    highlight art:lute art:hymnbook

    camera reset true

So far, everything says learning, wealth and power — and then there is the floor.
    highlight w_floor

    erase w_heavens w_earth w_floor

    camera on ["art@[0.05,0.62,0.9,0.38]"]

Something long and grey lies across it, at a slant.
    focus art:smear strength strong rest grey

    quiz "What is the long grey shape on the floor? Pick one." on_canvas true buttons_at.x 440 buttons_at.y 420
        right: A skull — stretched so far that, from the front, it is only a smear.
        wrong: Think of something this painting wants you to remember.
        * A shadow
        + A skull
        * A bone

    camera on ["art:smear"]

Stand close to the painting's right-hand edge and look (@draw skull@) along it, and the smear snaps into a skull.

    pause 0.8

The trick is called anamorphosis: a picture stretched so it only looks right from one spot.
    erase skull

    camera on ["art:hat_badge"] zoom 9

And it isn't the only skull: Dinteville wears a tiny one as a badge on his hat.
    highlight art:hat_badge effect ring

    camera reset true

    ask "Where is the hidden cross? Tap it." spot crucifix on art
        intro: One more thing hides here, half behind the green curtain.
        right: There it is: a crucifix, almost pushed out of the picture.
        wrong: Look in the top left corner, behind the curtain's edge.

    camera on ["art:crucifix"] zoom 4

Christ on the cross, squeezed into the corner: the one thing in the room that looks past death.
    highlight art:crucifix effect ring

    camera reset true

So the painting is built in layers: what the men know and own, a world falling out of tune, death on the floor, and, barely in view, the promise of the cross.
    highlight art:dinteville art:upper_shelf art:lower_shelf art:smear art:crucifix

Look straight at the ambassadors and you see their success; step to the side, and the same picture tells you that none of it lasts.
    highlight art:smear

    quiz "A photographer wants a portrait of a tech billionaire that quietly says success doesn't last. Which choice follows Holbein's method?"
        right: A hidden detail: like the skull, the message waits until the viewer moves.
        wrong: Holbein never says it outright — he makes you find it.
        * A caption: you will die
        + A detail that only shows from one angle
        * A gloomy face
        * A skull on his desk

So next time you stand in front of a painting, take a step to the side. Holbein expected you to.
    highlight art:floor

